---
description: Implement proper data anonymization and pseudonymization techniques
alwaysApply: true
---

data-anonymization:

- Anonymize data when full PII is not needed
- Use pseudonymization for data that needs to be reversible
- Remove or hash direct identifiers
- Aggregate data to prevent re-identification
- Apply k-anonymity, l-diversity, or differential privacy where appropriate
- Document anonymization techniques used
- Regularly test for re-identification risks

Anonymization techniques:
- Hashing: One-way transformation of identifiers
- Generalization: Replace specific values with ranges
- Suppression: Remove identifying attributes
- Perturbation: Add noise to data
- Aggregation: Combine individual records

Good implementation:
```javascript
// Anonymize for analytics
function anonymizeUserData(user) {
  return {
    userId: hash(user.id), // Pseudonymized
    ageRange: getAgeRange(user.age), // Generalized
    city: user.city, // Keep for regional analysis
    // Remove: name, email, phone, address
    activityCount: user.activities.length
  };
}

// K-anonymity for data export
function applyKAnonymity(dataset, k = 5) {
  return dataset
    .groupBy(['city', 'ageRange'])
    .filter(group => group.length >= k)
    .map(anonymizeGroup);
}
```

Bad practices:
```javascript
// Insufficient anonymization
const analytics = {
  userId: user.id, // Direct identifier
  email: user.email, // PII
  exactAge: user.age // Too specific
};

// Reversible "anonymization"
const masked = user.email.replace(/@.*/, '@***'); // Still identifiable
```
